{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's case hinges on 'a lossy compression cannot fully reconstruct the original signal; thus, the checklist's output is necessarily degraded relative to the expert's internal model.' But the relevant comparison is not reconstruction of the expert's internal model; it is hitting the objectively verifiable answer. A checklist can discard information irrelevant to the answer and still match or exceed expert accuracy, so the lossiness premise does not entail underperformance on the task. The conclusion is a non sequitur relative to the stated outcome metric.","counterexample":["A checklist that retains only answer-determinative features and drops the expert's irrelevant associations can be lossy relative to the expert's representation yet yield equal or higher accuracy on verifiable outcomes."],"target":"b16139b528b61743ec344333a53da32e58fcedde881d406121ab803fa593d9b0"}]}